Implementation 

# 1. Install Tailscale on Raspberry Pi

Install Tailscale:


curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh

Start Tailscale:

sudo tailscale up
sudo tailscale status
sudo tailscale down  ### disconnect tailscale if you do not need it again 

Authenticate using the Tailscale account.

# 2. Enable IP Forwarding

Create a persistent configuration:

sudo nano /etc/sysctl.d/99-tailscale.conf

Add:

net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding = 1

Apply:

sudo sysctl --system

Verify:

cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward

Expected result: 1.

# 3. Configure Raspberry Pi as Exit Node and Subnet Router

Advertise the Raspberry Pi as an Exit Node and advertise the home subnet:

sudo tailscale set \
  --advertise-exit-node \
  --advertise-routes=192.168.1.0/24

Replace the subnet with the actual home network subnet.

Approve the Exit Node and subnet route in the Tailscale Admin Console:

https://login.tailscale.com/admin/machines

# 4. Install Tailscale on Ubuntu Server

Install Tailscale on Ubuntu Server:

curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh

Authenticate:

sudo tailscale up

Retrieve the Tailscale IP:

tailscale ip -4

This allows direct access to the Ubuntu server using its private Tailscale address.

# 5. Configure Remote Access from Mobile Phone

Install the Tailscale application on the mobile phone.

Authenticate using the same Tailscale account.

Connect to the Raspberry Pi using the Exit Node option.

The mobile phone can now:

Route internet traffic through the home network.

Access the home LAN using its private IP addresses.

Connect to Ubuntu Server through SSH.

Access self-hosted applications.

Example SSH connection:

ssh username@192.168.1.X

# 6. Configure Nginx Proxy Manager

Nginx Proxy Manager was used to provide domain-based access to self-hosted applications.

Instead of accessing services using IP addresses and port numbers, internal domain names were configured.

Example:

https://portainer.example.internal
https://grafana.example.internal
https://nextcloud.example.internal
https://jellyfin.example.internal

For each application, configure a Proxy Host in Nginx Proxy Manager:

Domain Name: Internal application hostname.

Scheme: HTTP or HTTPS depending on the backend.

Forward Hostname/IP: Application hostname or reachable container IP.

Forward Port: Application's actual service port.

Websockets Support: Enable where required.

# 7. Configure Private DNS

Configure private DNS records pointing the application hostnames to the Ubuntu Server's LAN IP.

For remote access, configure Tailscale DNS with a restricted nameserver (split DNS) so the mobile phone can resolve the internal domain names.

The DNS server must be reachable through the Tailscale subnet route.

# 8. Configure HTTPS

Configure HTTPS certificates through Nginx Proxy Manager.

DNS-based certificate validation can be used with Cloudflare DNS.

This allows internal services to use HTTPS without requiring public access to the application ports.
